# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
james-will-4.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 2 ways: james-will-4.logic.r1 has nothing from it; james-will-4.logic.r1.2 and james-will-4.logic.r1.3 have "¬fulfilled_hopes"; they differ when only "as an experimenting body, the S. P. R. has completely fulfilled the hopes of its founders" is true, which the first allows
james-will-4.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered no, which keeps james-will-4.logic.r1.2 and james-will-4.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "as an experimenting body, the S. P. R. has completely fulfilled the hopes of its founders" is true. Is S2, "As an experimenting body, it cannot be said to have completely fulfilled the hopes of its founders.", true then?
james-will-4.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: james-will-4.logic.r1 and james-will-4.logic.r1.2 have "subjects_few ∧ work_slow" and "subjects_few ∧ work_slow → ¬fulfilled_hopes"; james-will-4.logic.r1.3 has "subjects_few ∧ work_slow"; they differ when only "as an experimenting body, the S. P. R. has completely fulfilled the hopes of its founders", "the clairvoyant and other subjects who will allow themselves to be experimented upon are few and far between" and "work with such subjects takes an immense amount of time and has had to be carried on at odd intervals by members engaged in other pursuits" are true, which the second allows
james-will-4.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps james-will-4.logic.r1 and james-will-4.logic.r1.2, to: Suppose "as an experimenting body, the S. P. R. has completely fulfilled the hopes of its founders", "the clairvoyant and other subjects who will allow themselves to be experimented upon are few and far between" and "work with such subjects takes an immense amount of time and has had to be carried on at odd intervals by members engaged in other pursuits" are true. Is S3, "The reasons for this lie in two circumstances: first, the clairvoyant and other subjects who will allow themselves to be experimented upon are few and far between; and, secondly, work with them takes an immense amount of time, and has had to be carried on at odd intervals by members engaged in other pursuits.", true then?
james-will-4.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: james-will-4.logic.r1 has "records_fallible → lit_worthless" and "lit_worthless → indispensable"; james-will-4.logic.r1.2 has "lit_worthless → indispensable"; they differ when only "the records of the facts cited in the literature of the supernatural are so fallible and imperfect that at most they lead to the opinion that a window may be kept open on that quarter" is true, which the second allows; james-will-4.logic.r1.3 has "lit_worthless ∧ bureau_work → indispensable"
james-will-4.logic.r1: argue/readings: U1: james-will-4.logic.r1 takes for granted "if the records of the facts cited in the literature of the supernatural are so fallible and imperfect that at most they lead to the opinion that a window may be kept open on that quarter, then the existing literature of the supernatural is practically worthless for evidential purposes"; james-will-4.logic.r1.2 and james-will-4.logic.r1.3 do not
james-will-4.logic.r1: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps james-will-4.logic.r1, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if the records of the facts cited in the literature of the supernatural are so fallible and imperfect that at most they lead to the opinion that a window may be kept open on that quarter, then the existing literature of the supernatural is practically worthless for evidential purposes"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
